import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.D_ZOUi6P.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Code review","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/code-review.md","filePath":"development/code-review.md","lastUpdated":1792785610000}'),i={name:"development/code-review.md"};function r(s,e,c,d,l,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="code-review" tabindex="-1">Code review <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code review&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page covers the guidelines we use when reviewing changes, whether the reviewer is a human or an AI. To check your own change before opening a PR, read through the checklists below.</p><h2 id="how-ai-review-works" tabindex="-1">How AI review works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-ai-review-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How AI review works&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A collaborator with write access can comment <code>/dd-review</code> on a PR to request an AI code review. The review uses the <code>codereview_guideline.md</code> files in the repository as its instructions:</p><ul><li>The file at the repository root applies to every PR.</li><li>A file in a subdirectory applies only to PRs that change files under that subdirectory.</li><li>The guidelines are read from <code>main</code>, not from the PR, so changes to them take effect after they merge.</li></ul><p>The review action removes <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files before the review runs, so each guideline file <strong>MUST</strong> stay self-contained, rather than pointing to other pages. This makes the guideline files one of the few exceptions to <a href="./style-guide#where-documentation-lives">Where documentation lives</a>. To add review guidance for one area of the codebase, add a <code>codereview_guideline.md</code> file in that area&#39;s directory and include it on this page.</p><p>The sections below render the guideline files as they&#39;re written.</p><h2 id="general-guidelines" tabindex="-1">General guidelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#general-guidelines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;General guidelines&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From <code>codereview_guideline.md</code>:</p><p>You are acting as a reviewer for a proposed code change made by another engineer. Focus on issues that impact correctness, performance, security, maintainability, or developer experience, in roughly that order: correctness comes first, then performance. Flag only actionable issues introduced by the pull request. When you flag an issue, provide a short, direct explanation and cite the affected file and line range. Prioritize severe issues and avoid nit-level comments unless they block understanding of the diff. Do not flag formatting or lint issues that <code>make fmt</code> and <code>make check-all</code> already enforce. After listing findings, produce an overall correctness verdict (&quot;patch is correct&quot; or &quot;patch is incorrect&quot;) with a concise justification. Ensure that file citations and line numbers are exactly correct using the tools available; if they are incorrect your comments will be rejected.</p><h2 id="project-specific-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">Project-specific review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-specific-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project-specific review checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Saluki is a Rust toolkit for building observability data planes. Its primary artifact, <code>bin/agent-data-plane</code>, runs alongside the Datadog Agent.</p><h3 id="generic-crates-must-stay-generic" tabindex="-1">Generic crates must stay generic <a class="header-anchor" href="#generic-crates-must-stay-generic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generic crates must stay generic&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Crates under <code>lib/saluki-*</code> (including <code>lib/saluki-io</code>) are general-purpose infrastructure. Flag code, comments, or documentation in those crates that refer to the Datadog Agent by name, to internal Datadog codenames or project names, or to Datadog-specific deployment topologies. This rule does not apply to <code>bin/agent-data-plane</code>, <code>lib/datadog-agent/</code>, configuration registry annotations, or known-configs documentation.</p><h3 id="generated-and-vendored-files" tabindex="-1">Generated and vendored files <a class="header-anchor" href="#generated-and-vendored-files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generated and vendored files&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Flag hand edits to files marked <code>@generated</code> or <code>DO NOT EDIT</code>; the source of truth must be changed and the file regenerated instead. Flag hand edits to the vendored Datadog schema under <code>lib/datadog-agent/config/schema/core/</code>, which must stay a pristine upstream copy.</p><h3 id="hot-paths-and-allocation" tabindex="-1">Hot paths and allocation <a class="header-anchor" href="#hot-paths-and-allocation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hot paths and allocation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Much of Saluki processes high volumes of metrics, logs, and traces. In per-event code paths, flag new heap allocations, clones, or string formatting that could be avoided, unbounded buffering or queues, and logging at any level that scales with event volume.</p><h3 id="concurrency-and-component-lifecycle" tabindex="-1">Concurrency and component lifecycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#concurrency-and-component-lifecycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Concurrency and component lifecycle&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Components run concurrently on Tokio and have explicit startup and shutdown. Flag blocking calls on async tasks, locks held across <code>.await</code> points, spawned tasks that are never joined or cancelled, and shutdown paths that can drop buffered data or hang. Changes to component lifecycle should have tests that exercise graceful shutdown.</p><h3 id="error-handling" tabindex="-1">Error handling <a class="header-anchor" href="#error-handling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Error handling&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Flag new <code>unwrap()</code>, <code>expect()</code>, or panicking indexing in non-test code where the failure is reachable from external input or configuration. Error messages should say what went wrong in plain language and, where relevant, name the configuration field involved.</p><h3 id="tests" tabindex="-1">Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Every property-based test function <strong>MUST</strong> be named with the <code>property_test_</code> prefix. The Make targets select tests by that prefix, so a misnamed property test silently runs in the wrong job.</li><li>Flag tests that cannot fail when the behavior under test regresses: no assertions, assertions only on values the test itself constructed, or a property test with no <code>prop_assert!</code>.</li><li>Flag tests that sleep to wait for background work instead of using deterministic synchronization or Tokio&#39;s paused time.</li><li>Test functions should be named as bare, descriptive sentences, without a <code>test_</code> prefix.</li></ul><h3 id="documentation-and-release-notes" tabindex="-1">Documentation and release notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#documentation-and-release-notes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Documentation and release notes&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>All public items <strong>MUST</strong> have rustdoc. Configuration fields <strong>MUST</strong> document what they control, their default value, and their edge cases.</li><li>If a pull request changes behavior but does not update the corresponding docs, comments, or doc strings, flag it.</li><li>User-visible changes to <code>agent-data-plane</code> need a release note fragment under <code>releasenotes/notes/</code>, unless the pull request carries the <code>changelog/no-changelog</code> label.</li></ul><h2 id="scoped-guidelines" tabindex="-1">Scoped guidelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#scoped-guidelines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scoped guidelines&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From <code>lib/datadog-agent/config/codereview_guideline.md</code>, which applies to PRs that change the configuration system:</p><h2 id="configuration-system-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">Configuration system review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration-system-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Configuration system review checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apply these when the pull request touches the Datadog configuration source model.</p><ul><li>Support for Datadog schema keys is classified in <code>schema/schema_overlay.yaml</code>. Flag changes that alter which keys are supported without updating the overlay, and flag code generated from the schema and overlay that was edited by hand instead of regenerated with <code>make build-schema-overlay</code>.</li><li>Files under <code>schema/core/</code> are a vendored upstream copy of the Datadog schema. Flag any hand edit to them.</li><li>A modeled key arrives in the Datadog Agent&#39;s canonical shape, and the source model must read that shape. Flag new <code>#[serde(rename)]</code> or <code>#[serde(alias)]</code> attributes on source model fields.</li><li>Whether a key is a Datadog key or a Saluki-only key depends on whether it appears in the schema, not on its name. Flag reasoning or code that infers the class of a key from its prefix, such as <code>data_plane.</code>.</li></ul>',31)])])}const p=a(i,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
